| **Figma trend guide** | https://www.figma.com/resource-library/web-design-trends/ | Currently documented visual/UI trends and their appropriate use cases; regularly recheck the article and compare with recent real sites. | Implement every trend, or claim that a dated trend list represents the latest state without checking. |

**Research order:** industry/product peers → Figma trends for current context → Behance/Awwwards for overarching visual language → Lapa Ninja for page architecture → Navbar Gallery for navigation → Footer Design for closing structure → Dribbble for bounded details → working live sites for validation. Tailor this order to the task; do not browse all sites for a tiny component edit.

Use precise search terms such as `[industry] website design 2026`, `[product type] editorial landing page`, `[industry] accessible pricing page`, `[product] dashboard data density`, `[brand tone] typography`, `[navigation requirement] navbar`, or `[audience] mobile experience`. Filter for fresh work when currency matters; distinguish publication date from last-modified or indexed date.

## 3. Anti-template / anti-“vibe-coded” pattern register (all 30 screenshot points)

The supplied screenshot lists 30 common telltales. **They are warning signals and review prompts, not universal design laws.** A real product can legitimately use cards, icons, gradients, dark colors, or motion. The problem is unmotivated, repeated, interchangeable execution. Apply the following rules:

| # | Screenshot pattern | Enforce this better alternative |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Harsh gradients** | Default to restrained surfaces. No generic purple→blue hero or decorative conic/radial glow. Use an intentional gradient only when justified by brand, imagery, or hierarchy, with accessible text contrast. |
| 2 | **Lucide icons everywhere** | Do not decorate each heading/card with the same generic outline icon. Use icons only when they clarify navigation, state, function or meaning; choose a coherent icon family and sizes. Lucide is acceptable for actual UI controls. |
| 3 | **Pure white on every section** | Build purposeful surface hierarchy using brand-tuned neutrals, paper/off-white, ink, tinted sections, photography or other art direction. Pure white remains appropriate for focused, clean interfaces. |
| 4 | **Rainbow coloring** | Avoid a different accent on every feature card. Use a controlled palette with semantic colors and consistent roles. Multiple vibrant colors are appropriate only when supported by brand or information encoding. |
| 5 | **Default drop shadows** | Prefer layout, whitespace and borders to convey grouping. Use restrained, physically coherent elevation only where layering or interaction actually requires it. No glowing every card. |
| 6 | **Three equal feature cards in a row** | Do not default to a universal three-column features strip. Choose layout based on content: narrative split, comparison, annotated product demo, editorial grid, case study, table, timeline or asymmetric feature sequence. |
| 7 | **Emojis as a design system** | Use real images, purposeful custom illustrations or consistent icons. Emojis only when audience, voice and context genuinely demand a playful style. Never substitute random emoji for interface clarity. |
| 8 | **Liquid glass / glassmorphism everywhere** | No indiscriminate blurred floating panels. Use translucency only with readable contrast, supported browsers and a specific functional purpose. |
| 9 | **Heavy-handed em dashes** | Avoid repetitive AI-like rhetorical punctuation and stylized copy. Use natural, varied sentence structure; ordinary punctuation is not prohibited. |
| 10 | **Inter / Geist / Space Grotesk as reflex** | Do not pick the same trendy font automatically. Choose typography for brand character, legibility, licenses and script coverage. Existing brand fonts take priority; these fonts remain legitimate options when appropriate. |
| 11 | **Colored left border on every card** | Avoid repeated accent sidebars that do not encode status. Use layout, typography and spacing first; reserve sidebars for actual categories, hierarchy, citation or state. |
| 12 | **Fake testimonials** | Never invent customers, reviews, quotes, logos, ratings or outcomes. Use verified proof; otherwise omit the section or add a clearly marked content placeholder for the owner. |
| 13 | **Default bento grid** | No gratuitous mismatched tiles to imply innovation. Use bento layouts only if item priority, content shape and reading order justify them; preserve mobile semantics. |
| 14 | **Decorative terminal window** | Avoid fake command-line screenshots unless the product genuinely provides developer workflows and the shown code/commands are accurate. Prefer real application demonstrations. |
| 15 | **“It's not X, it's Y”** | Avoid formulaic marketing copy. Say exactly what the product does, for whom, and what practical problem it solves. |
| 16 | **Checkmark bullets everywhere** | Use actual checklists only for checked requirements/features; otherwise use descriptive content, tables, illustrations, semantic lists, numbers, or no marker. |
| 17 | **Exactly three pricing tiers by default** | Price architecture must reflect actual packaging. Support one plan, two plans, usage-based pricing, custom quotes, enterprise contact or comparisons as warranted. Never fabricate plans or prices. |
| 18 | **No real product demo** | Prefer authentic UI captures, anonymized real product data, working interactive previews, diagrams or honest walkthroughs. Never depict imagined features as functioning production UI. |
| 19 | **Everything softly rounded** | Use a deliberate corner-radius system based on brand and control type. Square, restrained, mixed or rounded corners are valid when consistent; no universal oversized `rounded-3xl`. |
| 20 | **Purple/black everywhere** | Choose brand-specific colors with audience/domain fit. Do not auto-apply purple-on-charcoal “AI startup” styling. Purple or dark themes are valid if genuinely part of brand or task. |
| 21 | **No skeleton loaders** | Every async view needs intentional loading behavior. Use skeletons where layout is predictable and delays justify them; otherwise progress indicators or concise status messages may be better. |
| 22 | **Radial background orbs** | No gratuitous blurred circles behind every hero. Use imagery, composition, typographic scale, color blocking or real product visuals for focal interest. |
| 23 | **Dot-grid texture** | Do not sprinkle generic dots, grids or mesh textures as filler. Use a patterned background only where it has a meaningful relationship to brand or content. |
| 24 | **Sparkle icons** | Do not use sparkle/star icons as a generic indicator of “AI”, quality or magic. Describe a real capability and choose meaningful imagery. |
| 25 | **Animated arrows everywhere** | Movement should clarify next steps or interaction; no perpetual arrow bounce or gratuitous carousel/CTA motion. |
| 26 | **No Terms of Service** | For public products/services, provide a clear Terms link and required content when applicable. **Never invent legally binding terms**: use approved copy or mark it as pending legal review. |
| 27 | **No Privacy Policy** | Provide an accessible Privacy link and applicable notices when collecting personal data. Do not fabricate policy text, data-processing statements or consent requirements. Coordinate with compliance rules. |
| 28 | **Hover animations on everything** | Hover should indicate interactivity, not entertain. Use restrained transitions on interactive controls and visible keyboard-focus alternatives. Never rely on hover-only information. |
| 29 | **Neon coloring** | Avoid fluorescent accents unless truly brand-led and legible. Bright palettes and retrofuturist styling are optional artistic directions, not default SaaS themes. |
| 30 | **Generic pastel palette** | Do not automatically choose lavender/sky/peach/mint. Color should express the specific brand; pastels are fine for appropriate audiences and concepts. |

### Fast anti-slop refusal test

Before accepting a major new design, ask:
1. Could changing only the logo, product name and accent color make this an unrelated startup site? If yes, redesign the composition/content.
2. Is visual interest coming from meaningless glows, dots, sparkles or cards rather than real product substance? If yes, remove filler.
3. Is every section an interchangeable `heading + subtitle + three cards + CTA`? If yes, change information architecture.
4. Are icons, charts, testimonials, screenshots or numbers decorative rather than honest and meaningful? If yes, replace or omit.
5. Is the design coherent without animations, desktop-only effects and placeholder copy? If no, solve fundamentals first.
6. Does the site unmistakably reflect this brand's audience, product and voice? If no, iterate before declaring done.

## 4. 2026 Figma trends: evaluate, do not blindly implement

**Dated source baseline:** Figma, “Top Web Design Trends for 2026” — https://www.figma.com/resource-library/web-design-trends/. On substantial new design work, consult its latest available version (and compare with fresh real sites). The following is a decision framework, **not** a mandatory style bundle.

| Trend from Figma | When it can add real value | Guardrails / reject when |
|---|---|---|
| 1. **3D & immersive elements** | Product exploration, physical goods, spatial data, demonstrable interaction. | Heavy WebGL is gratuitous, breaks mobile performance, lacks a fallback, or hides important content. |
| 2. **Experimental navigation** | Small creative brands, narrative experiences and audiences that benefit from exploration. | Primary pathways become undiscoverable. Prioritize familiar patterns in high-stakes/utility apps. |
| 3. **Vibrant color palettes** | Expressive consumer, culture, lifestyle or distinctly bold brands. | Palette feels randomly colorful or fails contrast; do not default to neon. |
| 4. **Bold typography** | Strong brand voice, editorial storytelling, high-clarity hero/message hierarchy. | Body text becomes hard to read, decorative type delays content, or every word competes for attention. |
| 5. **Dark mode** | Focus-intensive products, media, developer tools, genuine user preference. | Was added merely to look premium; text and status contrast fail. Respect existing theme preference. |
| 6. **Motion & animation** | Transitions that reinforce hierarchy, feedback, continuity, and task progress. | Nonessential perpetual loops, motion sickness risks, excessive bundle size. |
| 7. **Gamified design** | Helpful onboarding, education, progress, optional engagement. | Manipulates decisions or distracts from consequential tasks. |
| 8. **Neumorphism** | Rare, highly controlled tactile details with clear affordances. | Low-contrast buttons, indistinguishable input states, fake depth everywhere. |
| 9. **Retrofuturism** | A brand with genuine historical/creative affinity for the style. | Chrome/neon/pixel art pasted onto an unrelated professional service. |
| 10. **Maximalism** | Visual storytelling, art, fashion, entertainment and intentional high-energy brands. | Information hierarchy, scannability, accessibility and performance collapse. |
| 11. **Collage** | Distinctive editorial, artistic, culture or personal storytelling. | Arbitrary cutouts/stickers masquerade as brand identity. |
| 12. **Neo-brutalism / anti-design** | Provocative, experimental brands with a clear art direction. | Confusing controls, poor contrast or inaccessible typography mistaken for edginess. |
| 13. **Sustainable web design** | All projects: lean assets/code, longevity, purposeful interactions, accessibility. | Never optional; use performance budgets and inclusive design. |

Figma also discusses AI chatbots, voice-activated interfaces and progressive lead nurturing. **These are product-feature options, not required visual trends.** Add AI/voice features only with a real user need, appropriate privacy/consent, tested capability and a user-requested scope. Never add a fake chatbot to make a page seem modern.

**Trend selection rule:** For a new visual direction, identify at most **one primary** expressive trend and **one supporting** trend, if any. Give each a brand/user justification. Defaults are quality typography, strong hierarchy, honest product evidence, restrained interaction and high usability—none requires novelty effects.

## 5. Design system: consistency without generic sameness

### 5.1 Tokens and existing conventions

- **Inspect and use existing project tokens first** (`tailwind.config.*`, CSS custom properties, theme files, design-system components). Never invent Tailwind classes, pretend tokens exist, or override the design system ad hoc.
- If tokens are missing and scope warrants it, create semantic primitives: `color.background`, `color.surface`, `color.text`, `color.text-muted`, `color.border`, `color.brand`, `color.action`, `color.focus`, `color.success`, `color.warning`, `color.danger`; `font.*`; `space.*`; `radius.*`; `shadow.*`; `motion.*`; `container.*`.
- Use CSS variables or the framework's existing theming conventions. Avoid unmaintainable inline styling; dynamic CSS custom properties are acceptable when appropriate. Do not introduce a new styling library solely for aesthetics.
- Base spacing on a coherent **4px/8px rhythm** but allow optical adjustments for typography, icons and exceptional layouts. Do not use random values everywhere or mandate one rigid gap for every context.
- Build a small family of intentional components (buttons, fields, tabs, cards if useful, navigation, alerts, empty states), not several nearly identical components with inconsistent geometry.
- Use contrast, text size, spacing, grouping and content—not arbitrary decorative lines—to create hierarchy.

### 5.2 Typography and editorial craft

- Select typefaces for brand, legibility, licenses and language support. Default to **one strong primary family** plus an optional purposeful second family, not two just because a template does.
- Define roles: display, H1/H2/H3, body, small/meta, controls and numeric data. Use a restrained modular type scale, variable `clamp()` sizing where helpful, deliberate line height, and consistent tracking.
- Avoid every headline being oversized, black and centered. Adjust by subject, page type, language length and viewport.
- Body content should be comfortable to read (commonly ~16–18px at standard desktop zoom, with suitable line height); keep long-form measures around **45–75 characters** where appropriate.
- Use readable line breaks; don't force `<br>` for a marketing composition that breaks at mobile widths. Do not capitalize, all-caps, or letter-space entire paragraphs for effect.
- Write real, concise, specific copy. Avoid filler: “Revolutionize”, “Seamlessly”, “Unlock your potential”, “Empower your journey”, “Built for the future”, and formulaic “not X, but Y”. Replace with real actions, constraints, results or benefits that are actually supported.

### 5.3 Palette, material and depth

- Start with the **existing brand palette**. If no brand exists, build a palette from a defined visual thesis and audience—not from AI default color presets.
- Default to a neutral foundation and **one primary action/accent** with separate functional status colors. A brand can legitimately have multiple accents if their roles are explicit and consistent.
- Avoid default purple-black, multicolor pastel, neon, harsh gradients, liquid glass and glow surfaces. Conditional exceptions require a reason connected to the brand, not the tool's taste.
- Build light/dark themes only when needed; make both fully legible and test state colors in each.
- Use no more than a purposeful set of corner radii and shadow levels. Every radius/shadow should communicate surface type or elevation rather than “modernity”.

### 5.4 Grids, spacing, composition

- Define readable content boundaries and consistent gutters. Use CSS Grid and Flexbox rather than absolute positioning for primary content.
- Favor **rhythm and variation**: dense then airy, typographic then visual, narrative then evidence. Keep underlying alignment intentional and visible even in asymmetric layouts.
- Choose a hero format that fits the actual message: clear editorial typography + honest image, compact utility introduction, split layout with working product view, search-led task entry, or visual narrative. **Never default blindly to a centered hero with a badge, vague two-line headline and two buttons.**
- Do not mechanically repeat `three cards in a row` for services, features, testimonials and benefits. Reflect actual content counts and importance.
- Prefer product-specific layouts over generic templates: real workflow diagrams, before/after states, authentic data tables, comparison matrices, case narratives, annotated screenshots, relevant demonstrations and real forms.
- On dashboards, prioritize legibility, density controls, navigation persistence, filter discoverability, table semantics and actionable information. Do not make every data feature a decorative card.

### 5.5 Imagery, icons and data visualization

- Prefer authentic, permissioned brand photography, real product screenshots, meaningful illustrations, factual graphics or intentional typography over generic 3D blobs and unrelated stock imagery.
- If a real screenshot is unavailable, clearly label any **illustrative mockup**. Do not render imagined product features as current functionality.
- Use optimized media with meaningful alt text when informative, empty alt when decorative, appropriate aspect ratios, focal point cropping and responsive variants.
- Icons are for meaning and usability. Keep stroke, weight, size and optical alignment consistent; do not make random icon grids into a fake feature story.
- Charts must show real or explicitly labeled sample data, axes/units where relevant, accessible labels and correct comparisons. Never fabricate KPIs or performance improvements.

## 6. Component and page-specific standards

### Navigation

- Study https://www.navbar.gallery/ for the **type of menu matching the site's information architecture**: simple bar, dropdown, mega menu, side nav, tabs, search, breadcrumbs, announcement rail, etc.
- Keep top-level labels clear, destinations useful, and active states visible. Support mouse, keyboard, touch, assistive technology and small viewports.
- Mega menus are for truly extensive hierarchies; avoid them for five links. Use a sticky header only when it helps browsing and does not consume excessive vertical space.
- Mobile menus must open/close predictably, be keyboard operable, manage focus, support escape/back behavior as appropriate, and not trap users behind an overlay.

### Hero and above the fold

- Explain **what the product/service is** and **what the user can do** in the first meaningful view; maintain a concrete action or clear next step.
- Distinctive design should derive from proposition + information order + art direction, not from a gradient globe.
- Prefer authentic hero proof: visualized product, actual outcomes (if evidenced), task entry, product photo or useful storytelling.
- Design for variable viewport heights; don't require users to scroll through 700px of empty hero to learn what the product does.

### Features, services, proof and demos

- Structure sections by real questions users have: How does it work? What do I get? Why trust it? What does it cost? What happens next?
- Avoid long stacks of interchangeable feature cards. Use meaningful demonstrations, diagrams, workflows, examples, comparisons or focused prose instead.
- Use **verified** logos, testimonials, metrics, awards, compliance references, customers, and case studies only. If absent, demonstrate value honestly with the product itself or label missing proof for follow-up.
- Use representative sample data only when clearly marked as illustrative and safe to show.

### Pricing and conversion

- Show only actual offering structures; do not fabricate three plans or highlight a “most popular” tier without evidence.
- Make the total price/terms legible. Clarify recurring periods, limitations, what happens after the CTA, and appropriate disclaimers.
- Keep form fields proportionate to the user's task; use proper labels, validation, keyboard/autofill support and clear post-submit feedback.
- Avoid false urgency, deceptive button text, prechecked consent, hidden fees or visual dark patterns.

### Footer

- Inspect https://www.footer.design/ for examples of **useful footer architecture**, not for copyable layouts.
- Include logical navigation categories, support/contact and owned social links where actually available, plus copyright/company details and Terms/Privacy/Cookie links **when required or applicable**.
- Use real destinations; do not insert dead links or dummy legal policies. If approval is required, create a clearly tracked requirement rather than pretending legal text exists.
- The footer should be a meaningful final navigation/conversion opportunity and visually consistent with the site, not an enormous empty black area.

### Application states and behavior

- For each async feature, design **initial/loading, empty, error, success, offline/network-failure and permission-denied states** as relevant. Avoid blank pages, infinite spinners and nonfunctional placeholders.
- Skeletons are optional but recommended for data-rich interfaces when the shape is stable; use readable state labels for unknown-duration work. Respect reduced motion.
- Define button/input/link states: default, hover (where supported), focus-visible, active/pressed, disabled, pending and validation/result states as relevant.
- Preserve users' input on recoverable failures. Show next steps after errors. Avoid double submissions; provide real feedback.

## 7. Accessibility is a design foundation, not a final touch

Target **WCAG 2.2 AA** where applicable and use semantic HTML. At minimum:

- Meet text contrast **4.5:1** for ordinary text and **3:1** for large text; relevant UI indicators and meaningful graphics generally require **3:1** non-text contrast. Do not rely on color alone to distinguish states.
- All controls must be navigable and usable with keyboard; show visible focus, proper labeling, skip links where relevant and logical heading/navigation order.
- Support 200% zoom and reflow to narrow screens without hiding content or creating unnecessary horizontal scroll. Respect user font scaling.
- Aim for generous touch targets (typically **44×44 CSS px** when layout permits) and adequate spacing; assess WCAG 2.2 target-size requirements rather than assuming every tiny icon is fine.
- Provide form labels, clear instructions, error association, sufficient time and predictable interactions. Do not use placeholder text as the only label.
- Support `prefers-reduced-motion`; never make essential comprehension depend on animation or hover. Provide pause controls for qualifying auto-moving content.
- Check accessible names for icons, cards, disclosures, dialogs and navigation. Ensure focus is returned after closing overlays.
- Avoid decorative effects that compromise text readability or hide content in forced-colors/high-contrast settings.

## 8. Responsive composition, not desktop shrinkage

- Design mobile, tablet and desktop as **intentional compositions** sharing the same system. Test representative widths (e.g., 320/360/390/768/1024/1440px and larger if relevant); adjust to actual project requirements.
- Mobile navigation, reading order, horizontal overflow, table strategies, fixed CTAs, touch targets, typography and image cropping require independent review.
- Use content-driven breakpoints rather than forcing all sections into the same responsive grid. Let CSS grid/flex wrapping handle variable content naturally.
- A desktop editorial split may become stacked on mobile; preserve semantic order and priority rather than hiding half the message.
- Test real text lengths, localization, empty states, validation messages, reduced motion, portrait/landscape and small-height viewports.
- Sticky/fixed elements must not obscure controls or significantly reduce usable screen space.

## 9. Motion: functional and deliberate

- Design motion to signal cause/effect, hierarchy, continuity or meaningful status. Prefer quick transitions with stable easing over perpetual animation.
- Use an explicit motion vocabulary (for example fast feedback / standard state change / occasional deliberate reveal) and make the chosen durations consistent.
- If animation is not improving comprehension, feedback or the brand's specific story, do not add it.
- Do not use bouncing CTAs, auto-scrolling carousels, scroll hijacking, parallax layers, animated cursors or decorative text morphing by default.
- Transform/opacity animation is generally preferable to layout-thrashing effects. Do not ship heavyweight motion libraries for minor hover transitions.
- Set appropriate `prefers-reduced-motion` fallbacks, avoid seizure-triggering flashes, and maintain control usability without animation.

## 10. Production-minded implementation and performance

- Reuse the project's components, tokens, framework, routes and build process. Avoid unnecessary package installation and theme-system duplication.
- Implement semantic DOM and responsive CSS; keep structure maintainable. Prefer design tokens and component abstractions over scattered inline style blocks and arbitrary one-off classes.
- Use appropriately sized WebP/AVIF images where supported, image dimensions/aspect ratios, lazy loading below the fold and intentional hero-image loading. Do not load 4K photos for tiny cards.
- Be judicious with web fonts: few families/weights, correct subsets, sensible fallbacks, `font-display` strategy and licensing.
- Limit layout shift, unnecessary network requests, render-blocking dependencies and expensive scroll effects. Evaluate Core Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, INP) where tooling is available rather than guessing that the page is fast.
- Confirm loading/empty/error and focus interactions in the actual browser when tools are available. If no browser/device testing occurred, explicitly say **not verified**.
- Do not silently rewrite brand identity, legal content, service descriptions or core user flows during visual cleanup.

## 11. Required quality gates for each substantial UI task

### Gate A — Before implementation
- [ ] Existing project brand assets/styles and adjacent pages inspected.
- [ ] User/audience/task and product-specific visual thesis established.
- [ ] Appropriate references considered; current sources consulted **when tools permit**.
- [ ] Relevant trends justified; no effect included solely because it is fashionable.
- [ ] Content, product data, images and social proof distinguished as verified, illustrative or missing.

### Gate B — After first implementation
- [ ] Real, specific content; no generic SaaS headlines and filler.
- [ ] Brand-specific composition; not copy-paste `badge → centered hero → 3 cards → bento → 3 prices → CTA`.
- [ ] Clear typography hierarchy, palette roles, token consistency and well-considered rhythm.
- [ ] Useful navigation and appropriate footer; real links and clear CTAs.
- [ ] Real product evidence or transparently labeled visualizations; no invented testimonials.
- [ ] No unnecessary gradients, purple glows, radial orbs, dots, sparkles, terminal mockups or hover theatrics.
- [ ] Important empty/loading/error/permission states implemented where relevant.
- [ ] Responsive layouts and keyboard/focus behavior accounted for.
- [ ] Legal/privacy requirements not hidden or fabricated.

### Gate C — Visual review and iteration

If browser/screenshot/test capabilities are available:
1. View at mobile, tablet and desktop widths; inspect the actual render, not only the source code.
2. Test real content, long titles, no data, failed network requests, focus order and touch interactions.
3. Compare against the **brief and reference principles**, not pixel-copy similarity.
4. Fix clipping, contrast, hierarchy, spacing, awkward line breaks, overused card patterns and unintentional template signals.
5. Recheck after fixes. A design review is incomplete until the resulting page is inspected again.

If visual tools are not available, do a source-level review, state which visual/device checks remain unverified, and provide a targeted manual test list rather than claiming perfect appearance.

### Gate D — Final self-assessment

Score each category from **0–5** (0 = absent, 5 = excellent). Aim for at least **4** in each category before calling a substantial design complete; do not average away a failure in usability or truthfulness.

| Category | Ask |
|---|---|
| Brand distinctiveness | Would this still feel like our brand after the logo was hidden? |
| Content credibility | Are claims, examples, metrics and social proof accurate and specific? |
| Information architecture | Can a first-time visitor find and understand the primary task quickly? |
| Editorial craft | Are typography, spacing, proportions and imagery deliberately art-directed? |
| Interaction quality | Do menus, controls and feedback behave coherently without gimmicks? |
| Accessibility | Can keyboard, screen-reader, mobile and low-vision users complete key tasks? |
| Responsive quality | Does every key layout adapt without overflow or loss of meaning? |
| Performance & robustness | Are assets lean and UI states/loading resilient? |

**Do not declare “production-ready,” “pixel-perfect,” “WCAG-compliant,” or “tested across browsers” unless that claim is supported by actual checks.**

## 12. What to report to the user after a meaningful design change

Keep the summary concise and factual:

1. **Design direction:** the visual thesis and target audience fit.
2. **Inspiration used:** links plus precisely which principles were borrowed; include inspection date if researched live.
3. **What changed:** layout, typography, color/tokens, components, imagery, copy and interaction.
4. **Anti-slop review:** generic patterns avoided or intentionally retained with reason.
5. **Quality checks:** exact widths/devices, accessibility checks, browser tests and performance tools *actually* used.
6. **Open items:** real assets/content/claims, legal review, missing screens, unverified tests and any tradeoffs.

For small changes, this can be two or three sentences. Do not overwhelm the user with a full trend essay on every button edit.

